## 4. Code Implementation Guidelines (Strict)

Follow these rules when writing code:

### 4.1 Early Returns
- Use early returns whenever possible to make the code more readable.
- Exit early from functions when conditions are not met.

```typescript
// ✅ Good - Early return
const MyComponent = ({ user }: Props) => {
  if (!user) return null;
  if (user.isLoading) return <Loading />;
  
  return <UserProfile user={user} />;
};

// ❌ Bad - Nested conditionals
const MyComponent = ({ user }: Props) => {
  return (
    <>
      {user ? (
        user.isLoading ? <Loading /> : <UserProfile user={user} />
      ) : null}
    </>
  );
};
```

### 4.2 Styling with Tailwind
- Always use Tailwind CSS classes for styling HTML elements.
- **NEVER** use inline CSS styles or `<style>` tags.
- Use `cn()` utility from `@/shared/utils/helpers` for conditional classes.

```typescript
// ✅ Good - cn() for conditional classes
import { cn } from "@/shared/utils/helpers";

<div className={cn(
  "flex items-center gap-2",
  isActive && "bg-primary text-white",
  isDisabled && "opacity-50 cursor-not-allowed"
)} />

// ❌ Bad - Ternary in className
<div className={`flex items-center gap-2 ${isActive ? "bg-primary text-white" : ""}`} />

// ❌ Bad - Inline styles
<div style={{ display: "flex", alignItems: "center" }} />
```

### 4.3 File Naming & Imports
- **ALL files MUST use kebab-case** (e.g., `login-button.tsx`, `auth-slice.ts`)
- **NO barrel exports** - Do NOT use `index.ts` files
- Import directly from the source file

```typescript
// ✅ Good - Direct imports with kebab-case files
import { LoginButton } from "@/features/auth/components/login-button";
import { useAuth } from "@/shared/hooks/use-auth";
import { cn, formatCurrency } from "@/shared/utils/helpers";
import { GoldPriceTicker } from "@/features/market/components/gold-price-ticker";

// ❌ Bad - Barrel imports (index.ts)
import { LoginButton, UserMenu } from "@/features/auth";
import { useAuth } from "@/shared/hooks";
import { cn } from "@/shared/utils";

// ❌ Bad - PascalCase or camelCase file names
import { LoginButton } from "@/features/auth/components/LoginButton";
import { useAuth } from "@/shared/hooks/useAuth";
```

### 4.4 Variable & Function Naming
- Use descriptive variable and function names.
- Event handler functions MUST have `handle` prefix.
- Use camelCase for variables and functions.
- Use PascalCase for components and types.

```typescript
// ✅ Good - Descriptive names with handle prefix
const handleClick = () => { ... };
const handleSubmit = () => { ... };
const handleInputChange = (e: React.ChangeEvent<HTMLInputElement>) => { ... };
const handleKeyDown = (e: React.KeyboardEvent) => { ... };

// ❌ Bad - Non-descriptive names
const onClick = () => { ... };
const submit = () => { ... };
const change = () => { ... };
```

### 4.5 Arrow Functions & Types
- Use `const` with arrow functions instead of function declarations.
- Always define TypeScript types for function parameters.

```typescript
// ✅ Good - Arrow function with type
type HandleClickProps = {
  id: string;
  action: "edit" | "delete";
};

const handleClick = ({ id, action }: HandleClickProps) => {
  // ...
};

// ❌ Bad - Function declaration
function handleClick(id, action) {
  // ...
}
```

### 4.6 Accessibility (a11y)
- Interactive elements MUST have proper accessibility attributes.
- Use semantic HTML elements (`<button>`, `<nav>`, `<main>`, etc.).
- Required attributes for interactive elements:
  - `aria-label` for icon-only buttons
  - `tabIndex={0}` for non-button clickable elements
  - `onKeyDown` handler for keyboard navigation
  - `role` attribute when semantic element isn't used

```typescript
// ✅ Good - Accessible button
<button
  onClick={handleClick}
  onKeyDown={handleKeyDown}
  aria-label="Sepete ekle"
  disabled={isLoading}
>
  <ShoppingCart className="h-4 w-4" />
</button>

// ✅ Good - Accessible clickable div (when button isn't suitable)
<div
  role="button"
  tabIndex={0}
  onClick={handleClick}
  onKeyDown={(e) => e.key === "Enter" && handleClick()}
  aria-label="Ürünü seç"
>
  ...
</div>

// ❌ Bad - Missing accessibility
<div onClick={handleClick}>
  <ShoppingCart />
</div>
```

### 4.7 Component Structure
- Components should follow this order:
  1. Imports
  2. Types/Interfaces
  3. Component definition
  4. Hooks (useState, useEffect, custom hooks)
  5. Derived state / memoized values
  6. Event handlers
  7. Early returns (loading, error states)
  8. Main render

```typescript
"use client";

import { useState, useMemo } from "react";
import { Button } from "@/shared/ui/button";
import type { User } from "./types";

interface UserCardProps {
  user: User;
  onEdit: (id: string) => void;
}

export const UserCard = ({ user, onEdit }: UserCardProps) => {
  // 1. Hooks
  const [isExpanded, setIsExpanded] = useState(false);
  
  // 2. Derived state
  const fullName = useMemo(() => `${user.firstName} ${user.lastName}`, [user]);
  
  // 3. Event handlers
  const handleToggleExpand = () => {
    setIsExpanded((prev) => !prev);
  };
  
  const handleEditClick = () => {
    onEdit(user.id);
  };
  
  // 4. Early returns
  if (!user) return null;
  
  // 5. Main render
  return (
    <div className="rounded-lg border p-4">
      <h3>{fullName}</h3>
      <Button onClick={handleEditClick} aria-label="Kullanıcıyı düzenle">
        Düzenle
      </Button>
    </div>
  );
};
```